{
  "dom_id": "step-q109733",
  "name": "Question 4",
  "problem": "The vector-valued function $r(x,\\theta )$ parametrizes the surface of revolution generated by rotating the curve $y=\\sqrt{x-2}$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [3,6].$ Which of the following could be $r?$",
  "worked_solution": "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/foundations/14275690/assets/56a380b075d10862170ed9c9a45c07dddff3eaef1ea9fb3109a61cc3d27b5ac4.png)\n\n \n\nA point $(x,y,z)$ on the surface generated by rotating the graph of $y=f(x)$ by $2\\pi $ radians about the $x$-axis for $x\\in [a,b]$ has the position vector\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}r=⟨x,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,f(x)\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [a,b],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFirst, notice that $y=f(x)=\\sqrt{x-2}>0$ for all $x\\in [3,6].$ \n\nSo, the parametrization of our surface of revolution is \n\n$$\nr(x,\\theta )=⟨x,\\,\\sqrt{x-2}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\theta ,\\,\\sqrt{x-2}\\operatorname{sin}⁡\\theta ⟩,\\,x\\in [3,6],\\,\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi ).\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
  "fields": [
    {
      "key": "selection",
      "type": "radio",
      "choices": [
        {
          "option": "a",
          "dom_id": "questionWidget-choiceLetterCircle-109733-a",
